   "confidence": "low",
   "notes": "Sedgwick Building (T.G. Jackson, 1904), Downing St front: dark red-brown brick with buff Ketton/Clipsham stone dressings, Jacobean. Ground floor of round-arched windows with stone voussoirs over a stone plinth behind a low wall; first floor tall two-light mullion-and-transom windows; second floor shorter cross windows; steep tiled roof with gabled dormers above a corbelled cornice. A stone doorway with columns, broken pediment and carved tympanum (fourth bay from the east; the main museum entrance with the carved beasts lies further west, beyond this wall). 12 bays spaced evenly over the 49 m wall; window rhythm estimated from raking Mapillary views along Downing St and Commons photos."
  },
